{"ok":true,"total":3,"succeeded":3,"failed":0,"concurrency":3,"durationMs":58623,"results":[{"id":"2-kintsugi-C","ok":true,"path":"/Users/jhuang/Desktop/JH-Projects/risk3d/_claude/themes-nightink/2-kintsugi-C.png","sourcePath":"/Users/jhuang/.codex/generated_images/01a0e8d1-c2df-71a3-a958-86cdd18773aa/exec-abadcbcc-1b31-42a9-bdc5-6a0e2c22f40b.png","revisedPrompt":"Create exactly one final raster image: a tall 9:19.5 portrait smartphone video-game screenshot for a fictional calm world-conquest strategy board game titled “War Table,” inspired by the broad board-game structure of classic territorial conquest games but with an original visual identity.\n\nShow a recognizable world map including the Americas, Europe, Africa, Asia, and Australia. Divide the land into clearly readable territories owned by four players. The map should fill most of the screen while retaining generous breathing room.\n\nArt direction: a single cohesive, restrained “indigo and kintsugi” design system combining a sophisticated dark night-ink app palette with Japanese sumi-e principles. Smooth deep indigo-navy background. Coastlines rendered as expressive tapered brush strokes in soft ivory ink, swelling and thinning organically, with subtle dry-brush texture at stroke endings. Internal borders BETWEEN territories are delicate fine gold seams, like kintsugi repair lines. Territory ownership uses soft, low-saturation mineral ink washes in exactly four families: muted coral, slate blue, pale gold, and sage; washes softly fade at edges with bokashi character while remaining distinguishable. Add only a few sparse, elegant calligraphic brush strokes suggesting ocean waves. Use the zen ensō circle as the recurring marker motif.\n\nCritical board constraint: exactly ONE marker per territory—one small, legible serif troop number centered inside one small brushed gold ensō circle. No additional icons, pieces, flags, labels, or marks inside territories. Avoid overlaps and duplicate markers.\n\nUI: At the very top, four tiny understated player markers, one for each ownership color, with ample spacing. Use one elegant high-contrast serif typeface throughout, visually similar to Cormorant Garamond. At the bottom, a minimal refined pill control containing exactly these labels in order: “Place   Attack   Fortify   End”. Make only “Attack” the single active gold-accented element. Include one separate compact “Blitz” button nearby, visually subordinate and not active. Gold is reserved for hairline territory seams, the brushed ensō markers, and the single active UI state; keep all other UI ivory or subdued.\n\nMood: extremely quiet, precious, jewel-like, contemplative, premium, high detail, crisp and readable at smartphone scale. Flat game screenshot presentation, no phone hardware mockup. No figures, no seals, no fake Asian characters, no decorative scenery clutter, no taglines, no watermark. Ensure all requested text is spelled correctly.","bytes":2583640,"durationMs":53128,"attempts":1,"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","modelProvider":"openai","reasoningEffort":"low","threadId":"01a0e8d1-c2df-71a3-a958-86cdd18773aa"},{"id":"3-lacquer-A","ok":true,"path":"/Users/jhuang/Desktop/JH-Projects/risk3d/_claude/themes-nightink/3-lacquer-A.png","sourcePath":"/Users/jhuang/.codex/generated_images/01a0e8d1-c332-7b02-bfce-282107f6027d/exec-fec92ea7-fcee-4956-ab42-07cccdb5c73c.png","revisedPrompt":"Create exactly one final raster image, a wide 16:9 premium desktop video-game screenshot for a fictional strategy board game titled “War Table.”\n\nShow the whole world board mid-game: a recognizable world map with the Americas, Europe, Africa, Asia, and Australia, subdivided into plausible board-game territories controlled by four players. Make the visual language exceptionally cohesive and minimalist.\n\nArt direction: “sumi on black lacquer.” The ground is deep indigo blue-black urushi lacquer with subtle physical depth, restrained reflections, and a soft sheen. Combine the disciplined palette of a refined dark night-ink app with Japanese sumi-e structure. Use only soft, low-saturation territorial ink washes in muted coral, slate blue, pale gold, and sage. The washes should use bokashi gradients, translucent pooling, and softly fading edges. Along coastlines, let grey-blue ink-wash mist glow subtly. Draw coastlines and borders as expressive pale-ink tapered brush strokes that naturally swell and thin, with delicate dry-brush texture at their ends. Add only a few sparse calligraphic wave strokes in the ocean. Sea routes are dotted with tiny irregular brush dabs.\n\nExactly ONE gameplay mark per territory: a single small brushed ensō circle containing one highly legible small serif troop number. Do not add any other tokens, icons, labels, symbols, pins, or repeated marks within territories. Use an elegant high-contrast serif typeface in the spirit of Cormorant Garamond for all text. Keep generous negative space and excellent readability.\n\nOne territory is selected: give it a brighter, precise brush outline with the ONLY gold maki-e accent on the map. From that selected territory, show exactly one fluid calligraphic brush-stroke arrow curving to an adjacent territory. Keep the arrow refined and unambiguous.\n\nBottom interface: a very thin gold maki-e horizontal rule with one small brushed ensō centered on the rule. Beneath it, a restrained rounded pill segmented control reading exactly: “Place   Attack   Fortify   End turn”, with “Attack” softly illuminated as the single active element. Include one small adjacent button labeled exactly “Blitz”. Gold is used only for hairline accents on this bottom UI rule and the active territory, plus the subtle active Attack state; avoid decorative gold elsewhere.\n\nNo human figures, armies, figurines, seals, stamps, fake Japanese characters, scenery, decorative clutter, excessive icons, extra copy, taglines, watermark, logos, or frame. Calm, contemplative, strategic, luxurious, tactile, high-detail, polished game UI. Ensure the world geography, territory boundaries, numbers, button labels, and selected attack interaction remain readable at screenshot scale.","bytes":2403190,"durationMs":58449,"attempts":1,"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","modelProvider":"openai","reasoningEffort":"low","threadId":"01a0e8d1-c332-7b02-bfce-282107f6027d"},{"id":"3-lacquer-C","ok":true,"path":"/Users/jhuang/Desktop/JH-Projects/risk3d/_claude/themes-nightink/3-lacquer-C.png","sourcePath":"/Users/jhuang/.codex/generated_images/01a0e8d1-c3e1-7460-9f6c-2b077d06f9b0/exec-e6408451-663f-48af-819a-ae7ed177d745.png","revisedPrompt":"Create exactly one final raster image: a tall portrait 9:19.5 smartphone video-game screenshot for a fictional game titled “War Table.” Show a calm, minimalist world-conquest strategy board game, recognizably inspired by classic territory-control board games but with wholly original UI and artwork. The recognizable world map includes the Americas, Europe, Africa, Asia, and Australia and fills most of the screen with generous breathing room. Divide the land into clearly legible territories owned by four players.\n\nUnify two aesthetics into one restrained premium design system:\n1) Dark “night ink” application palette and discipline: deep indigo–navy ground; soft, low-saturation territory tints in muted coral, slate blue, pale gold, and sage; gold reserved strictly for hairline accents and the single active territory; one elegant high-contrast serif typeface in the spirit of Cormorant Garamond; generous negative space.\n2) Japanese sumi-e ink structure: coastlines are expressive tapered pale-ink brush strokes that swell and thin, with subtle dry-brush texture at their ends; territory ownership colors appear as translucent soft ink washes fading at the edges (bokashi); sparse calligraphic brush strokes suggest waves in the ocean; recurring zen ensō brush circles.\n\nCritical map rule: EXACTLY ONE mark per territory—one small readable serif army number centered inside one small brushed ensō circle. No other icons or marks inside any territory. Keep territory borders clear enough to play while retaining the ink-wash style.\n\nVariant/material: sumi on black lacquer. Background is deep blue-black urushi lacquer with subtle depth and a soft sheen. Grey-blue ink-wash mist gradients softly glow along coastlines. Coastlines use pale ink brush strokes. Sea routes are dotted using tiny brush dabs. Gold maki-e hairline accents appear ONLY on one thin UI rule and the single active territory.\n\nUI structure: four tiny, understated player markers aligned at the top. Minimal bottom pill control with the exact readable labels “Place  Attack  Fortify  End”, plus one separate small “Blitz” button. The active element may use the lone gold accent. Keep all text crisp and correctly spelled. No figures, no stamps or seals, no fake Japanese characters, no scenery clutter, no tagline, no watermark, no extra panels. High detail, premium, cohesive, calm, legible mobile-game screenshot, edge-to-edge portrait composition.","bytes":2309031,"durationMs":50795,"attempts":1,"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","modelProvider":"openai","reasoningEffort":"low","threadId":"01a0e8d1-c3e1-7460-9f6c-2b077d06f9b0"}]}
